{"name":"Apatite Blue","hex":"#31827b","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"apatite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A calcium fluoride phosphate of variable composition, sometimes used in the manufacture of fertilizer, as a gemstone, and (in powdered form) as a pigment, and also produced biologically in bones and teeth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apatite"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Apatite","blocks":[{"text":"Apatite is a group of phosphate minerals, usually hydroxyapatite, fluorapatite and chlorapatite, with high concentrations of OH−, F− and Cl− ion, respectively, in the crystal. The formula of the admixture of the three most common endmembers is written as Ca10(PO4)6(OH,F,Cl)2, and the crystal unit cell formulae of the individual minerals are written as Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2, Ca10(PO4)6F2 and Ca10(PO4)6Cl2."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Apatite_on_Siderite_%2813156440584%29.jpg/330px-Apatite_on_Siderite_%2813156440584%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apatite"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Apatite_taill%C3%A9e.jpg/500px-Apatite_taill%C3%A9e.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Apatite_taill%C3%A9e.jpg/1280px-Apatite_taill%C3%A9e.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Apatite_taill%C3%A9e.jpg/500px-Apatite_taill%C3%A9e.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Faceted blue apatite, Brazil","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApatite_taill%C3%A9e.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Thin_section_microscopy_Siilinj%C3%A4rvi_R301_6170_apatite.jpg/500px-Thin_section_microscopy_Siilinj%C3%A4rvi_R301_6170_apatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Thin_section_microscopy_Siilinj%C3%A4rvi_R301_6170_apatite.jpg/1280px-Thin_section_microscopy_Siilinj%C3%A4rvi_R301_6170_apatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Thin_section_microscopy_Siilinj%C3%A4rvi_R301_6170_apatite.jpg/500px-Thin_section_microscopy_Siilinj%C3%A4rvi_R301_6170_apatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail",